"""
Real-time pulse extraction using the POS algorithm.

pos_algorithm(), bandpass_filter(), estimate_heart_rate(), calculate_snr(),
mean_skin_rgb(), landmarks_to_pixels(), build_face_landmarker(), and
ensure_face_landmarker_model() are copied VERBATIM and never touched by
any of the tracker/diagnostics work below.

# ===== ROI ARCHITECTURE CHANGE =====
Per explicit request, the rPPG ROI is now the union of four small
anatomical patches -- forehead, left cheek, right cheek, chin -- instead
of the previous whole-face-oval-minus-eyes/mouth mask. Each patch is a
square centered on a single stable MediaPipe landmark anchor (see
_ROI_ANCHOR_LANDMARKS), sized relative to detected face width so it
scales with camera distance. build_roi_mask() keeps its old signature
(returns (mask, bbox)) so mean_skin_rgb() and every downstream POS/
filter/HR/SNR call is completely unaffected -- only WHICH pixels feed
mean_skin_rgb() has changed, not how they are used afterward.

# ===== TRACKING ARCHITECTURE CHANGE =====
Flow: MediaPipe init -> ROI definition -> KLT -> (<=10% movement: KLT
continues) -> (>10% movement: ROI-only ECC realignment) -> update ROI
-> KLT resumes -> MediaPipe recovery only if KLT/ECC cannot maintain
the ROI.

  - MediaPipe runs only for the INITIAL detection and for genuine
    RECOVERY (severe feature loss, failed optical flow, failed ECC
    convergence, invalid ROI, face leaving frame). It is never
    triggered merely because movement exceeded 10%.
  - KLT (cv2.calcOpticalFlowPyrLK) tracks a set of feature points
    selected (cv2.goodFeaturesToTrack) across the whole face-oval region
    (build_tracking_mask(), unchanged from the prior tracker version) --
    NOT restricted to the four rPPG patches -- because flat cheek/
    forehead/chin skin is poor KLT material on its own; eyes/mouth/nose
    edges give far more reliable motion estimates. This tracking mask is
    used ONLY to estimate motion, never to select which pixels are
    averaged for the rPPG signal.
  - Every frame, the median DISPLACEMENT MAGNITUDE of the valid tracked
    points (np.median(norm(new_pts - old_pts)), not the median of the
    translation vector's components -- robust to a few bad optical-flow
    points) is normalized by the ROI diagonal (sqrt(w^2+h^2) of the
    union of the four ROI patches at last MediaPipe (re)detection):
    normalized_motion = median_displacement / roi_diagonal. Separately,
    the median translation VECTOR + mean-radial scale change of the same
    points gives one rigid (translation + uniform scale) transform,
    applied identically to all four ROI rectangles (assumes the face
    plane moves ~rigidly frame-to-frame at 30 fps) -- that transform is
    what actually moves the ROI; normalized_motion only decides KLT vs ECC.
  - If normalized_motion <= motion_threshold (default 0.05, i.e. 5% of
    the ROI diagonal; tunable via FaceROITracker(motion_threshold=...)
    / --ecc_motion_threshold, NOT a fixed pixel count), that transform is
    accepted directly ("KLT" frame).
  - If it exceeds motion_threshold, cv2.findTransformECC() is run SEPARATELY on each
    of the four small ROI patches (never on the whole frame or face) --
    template = last known-good patch pixels, input = a same-size crop of
    the CURRENT frame at the KLT-predicted candidate location. ECC only
    estimates a small residual translational correction to that
    location; it never supplies or alters pixel intensities. After
    ECC, KLT is RE-SEEDED from the corrected ROI patches and tracking
    resumes ("ECC" frame, occasional, not a continuous tracker).
  - If KLT loses too many points, or ECC fails to converge on a majority
    of the four patches, or the resulting ROI leaves the frame, that
    triggers a genuine MediaPipe recovery, which redefines all four ROIs
    from scratch and reseeds KLT.

Whichever mechanism supplied the current frame's ROI, the actual
rPPG pixel values are always read fresh, unmodified, from the raw
current frame at the final ROI location via the unmodified
mean_skin_rgb() -- ECC/KLT never interpolate, smooth, or otherwise
alter the measured pixel intensities themselves; they only decide WHERE
to look.

# ===== PERFORMANCE DIAGNOSTICS =====
The 10-second-window summary keeps every original line (MediaPipe
frames/usage, Re-detections, Tracker failures, actual vs nominal FPS,
HR, SNR) and additionally reports KLT frames/usage, ECC frames/usage,
KLT failures, and ECC activations/failures, per the requested
architecture. The final end-of-run summary reports the same, plus
MediaPipe re-detections and average actual FPS.

Requires: pip install mediapipe opencv-python scipy numpy
"""

